      "math_academy_id": "q-127462",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-0}{1}=\\frac{y-(-5)}{0}=\\frac{z-1}{9}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y+5}{0}=\\frac{z-1}{9},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{y+5}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=-5:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1+9t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{y+5}{0}$ part and instead write $y=-5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{9},\\,y=-5\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
